No, they didn't get a search warrant to search
every file on U of O computers.  The SPA got a tip
that the general access computing labs had copies
of software which was not licensed.  The information
was easily verifiable (just go to a computing lab,
and access a program on the HD, when you notice
that several HDs have programs with the same
serial number/owner name/etc, there is a clear
violation of Copyright).

The settlement was out of court, and part of the
agreement was for U of O to hold a conference on
the vagaries of software piracy, as well as making
a public appology.  However, no other details were
released by the SPA or U of O, to my knowledge...

U of O probably SAVED money, since the practice
had been going on for years. $130,000 doesn't pay
for that many $500 programs... U of O users
had probably pirated quite a bit more software
than the $130,000 fine indicates.

I think Open Systems Today did a little write-up
on the incident.  I have also seen other mini
write-ups in PC World, Personal Computing, etc.

I guess what is important here is that the SPA
was successful in getting U of O to settle out
of court, and the settlement was sizeable...
U of O wouldn't have settled if they thought
they could win the case, obviously. The other
possibility is that 130,000 was less than
aggressively pursuing the case in the courts.

>    3) alternatively, did the SPA intimidate the U. of Oregon into
>       seaching every file on every computer. If so, won't that
>       violate the same constitutional protections?

No again.  However, U of O does have a policy now
that allows them to inspect any University owned
machine for pirated software.  They also make
users sign use agreements that, supposedly,
would transfer liability to the offending user.

Most other OSSHE institutions are exploring using
the same type of agreements, and have really beefed
up their software piracy police...

