When bootleg copies of copyrighted material find their way onto an
anonymous ftp site, some means of protection should be there for archive's
maintainers.  The alternative would be manual screening of each upload;
this expense would doom the majority of ftp servers.

>Or a case of "it's my ftp server, my property and I'll log whatever I
>damn please and do whatever I want with the info - you're free to not
>connect"?

That's certainly a valid option.  Many sites use usage information to
justify expansion(s) and/or upgrade(s) for their systems.  

Of course, I'd be interested in the details of any *misuse* of this
info; of course, that's an indictment of that particuar site, not a 
global condemnation of the activity.

Do you feel as strongly about system accounting, which (on some systems)
provides a completely picture of your activities?  I can tell you right
now that upgrade/expansion money will NOT come my way unless I can docu-
ment the heavy use that justifies it.

What about the phone company?  They require personal information before
providing phone service, and they maintain near-complete histories of
your use of their service.  What's the difference?

