>contained in them.

This may have violated your constitutional and contractual right
to due process [see references].

>   I will not go into exactly was what in them, but there was 
>nothing that would be considered illegal.

If you were punished for the legal content of material, your
constitutional and contractual rights to freedom of expression may
have been violated.

>It has also come to my 
>attention that the system administrator has been discussing the files 
>with other people who had nothing to do with the administration of 
>our system or our department.

Your rights under the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act quite
likely have been violated (FERPA).

>What I want to know is, is this legal?  
>Can they just look through my files?  Is there any legal action I can 
>take against them?  

Legal action is very expensive. Less expensive is 1) talking to the
University omnibudsman 2) filing a formal complaint with the school 3)
filing a FERPA complaint.

