kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) wrote:
>>}How does this "inspection" differ from the general suspicionless
>>}searches that the 4th Amendment was suppose to protect against?
>
>[...] but it might protect individuals to whom which
>university-owned resources (offices, desks, dorm rooms, computers,
>lockers) have been assigned.

You are issued a PC by the university.  It is not your property; it
has merely been issued to your for your work.  If the software in-
stalled on that system comes from a university site license (as op-
posed to the copy you purchased for personal use), it belongs to the
University as well.

I think we'll agree that the university has the right (and, in fact,
the obligation) to ensure that its property is being maintained in
the proper fashion.  For a PC, that would include a check for soft-
ware outside the University's licenses (or illegal copies of Univer-
sity-owned software).  After all, it's *their* machine, not yours;
it's *their* software licenses, not yours.

I would equate a "legitimate software" check with a dormitory room
inspection or a laboratory inspection; the university has the right 
to ensure that you're using their equipment properly.

If I bring a PC from home, I wouldn't support a University search
*unless* they had reason to believe that I had installed University-
owned software without permission.  Asking me to call up the registra-
ation screen of, say, Quattro Pro is not unreasonable.  It's their
software, not mine.

Of course, you should *always* keep the license/purchase records of
your software, right?

--Wes

