>How does this "inspection" differ from the general suspicionless
>searches that the 4th Amendment was suppose to protect against?

Check with a lawyer for a more definitive answer, but I think that the
key point is that the 4th Amendment is supposed to make you secure
from suspicionless searches of YOUR property. But the statement above
is about "University owned machines", i.e. U of O's property not
YOUR property. Some liability undoubtedly lies with the real owner,
even if the owner let's you use something they own. They may, as part
of their agreement allowing to use their property pass responsibility
for lawful use of it onto you. But they may retain other rights of
usage for themselves to protect themselves from criminal liabilities.
Note that the 4th Amendement guarantees your right to be free from
unwarranted searches and seizures from the GOVERNMENT--it says
guarantees nothing about any private agreement you might enter into
with another property holder in order to use their property.

