Let's distinguish ethical and legal responsibilities here, then reintegrate 
them once it is clear that we see the confluence.  This guy (person) has your
password, and is authorized to use the computer with it during a certain 
period of time. At the end of that period, you terminate that priveledge.
Only in this case, your technologies didn't terminate that priveledge, and
those technologies represented your school's policy toward that student at 
that time (unless the student was told not to use the computer after a 
certain date, in which case you made merely a computer and not ethical error).
The student has continued to use the computer, but as a non-student now that
the period of his involvement with the school is over.  What activity would
 be ethical for him to take regarding the computer?  If he believed that it
was Ok to use the computer, he did not act unethically by doing so. However,
if you can demonstrate viably that he believed otherwise (eg. by acting in
a different way after Date X), then the legal question ensues.

At this point, the obvious response is: hang him.  The student violated laws,
protocols, etc. that are crucial to the integrity of student life and the
university's covenant with its community and the community at large. The 
student has a responsibility legally to the law regarding the violations, but
also (I think) has a responsibility to the university on its own terms. Why
can't you freeze his records? Pending his participation in the university's
investigation? This action seems appropriate.

Good luck. 

