This is another posting with permission of an article from the
newsletter _Measure_.  All articles are posted to alt.activism.d, and
certain of them are posted to other newsgroups that seem relevant.

["Student evaluation" at Stanford isn't exactly an innovation.  I'm
pretty sure it was done 30 years ago when I came to Stanford.  Sometimes
I agree with the "evaluation" of my teaching, and sometimes I
disagree, but I have never found it ill-motivated.  Apparently
this is not always the case. - jmc]

University Centers for Rational Alternatives
_Measure_ Number 112, January 1993

     WILL ACADEME TEACH AMERICA, OR AMERICA TEACH ACADEME?1

                           Michael Platt

        The innovation of "student evaluations" of teachers does not encourage
study; it expresses contempt for its highest goal, truth, the thing that
ought most to unite student and teacher; it blurs the fundamental
distinction between the student and the teacher, erasing the authority
of the teacher; and it has worked a revolution in Academe. These
"evaluations" also traduce justice, within Academe and may subvert it in
America.
        In order to understand how these "evaluations" treat people unjustly,
we have to start with what they first do to truth.
        From the start, the form and language of these "evaluations"
announces an indifference to the truth or the falsity of the claims
students make on them. The crucial thing is the tabulated rating of the
students' satisfaction with the teacher; whatever space is provided for
comments in prose is just stroking. That is the way they are set up and
the way they are judged. Consider an example. If a student damns a
history teacher with a "1" on a scale of "5," saying: "I don't know why
he is employed. His persistent criticism of the Catholic Church and all
its teaching offended me deeply" and if another student in the same
course also gives the teacher a "1," saying, "The guy should be fired.
His praise of the glories of the medieval Church was mindless,"--all that
amounts to in the tabulation is two votes for the teacher's dismissal.2
Although taken together the two prose protests add up to a _prima
facie_ case for the excellence of the teacher, since he seems to have
challenged two students each held fast by received, though opposed,
opinion, this _prima facie_ evidence of excellence matters not a stick
to those who identify learning with "satisfaction." After all, neither
student was "satisfied." Of course, a good teacher would say, "And the
fact they weren't satisfied is evidence of good teaching not bad," but
"student evaluations" were not invented by teachers.
        The new regime in the colleges really wants to have it both ways. They
want to say that student "satisfaction" is reliable evidence of student
learning, and that such student "learning" is reliable evidence of good
teaching. What this means for students, teachers, and the institution is
that learning is now understood as satisfaction, teaching as whatever
entertainment secures this satisfaction, that grading is a species of
flattery, and that truth has turned into opinion. This is clear. But when
colleges use the "results" of these "evaluations" against teachers, then
the claim must be 1) that students know about teaching; 2) that
students are fit judges of teachers and the best judges of their own
teachers, and 3) that what students say is true, whatever they say and
simply because they say it, true even when they speak anonymously,
indeed especially when they speak anonymously about their own
teachers.
        Most Americans have a different experience of justice. From growing
up in America, from playing games, from watching sports, from talking
to parents, from listening to the news, from reading detective novels,
from seeing movies, such as _Twelve Angry Men_, from watching
trials, from serving as jurors, and from study, most Americans have a
pretty good, if not a very fine or very detailed, knowledge of the ways
in which we Americans arrange for the truth to come out so that justice
be done. Trial by jury, _habeas corpus_, the Bill of Rights, rules of
evidence, and simply what a trial is like: with defendant, accuser,
judge, and jury--these are things Americans grow up knowing and
come to know better as they grow older. We should be thankful. Not
every country provides such an experience. Let us look at "evaluations"
in the light of that experience.
        What standing would the "students" who render "student evaluations"
have in an American court? The answer is none. It could not be said
that they knew about teaching. In an American court, only expert
witnesses are allowed to give their opinion about the professional
conduct of a defendant. To qualify as such, in an American court, you
have to have recognized experience, skill, training, certification, etc. If
you give testimony about a doctor's performance of his professional
duties, you have to be a doctor, or prove to the court you have medical
knowledge. Likewise for a dentist, a lawyer, a pilot, a mechanic, etc.
Thus, to have standing in an American court, the "student" would have
to be a teacher. No student could qualify.
        Supposing for a moment, however, that a student were admitted as an
expert witness, still the student's testimony would be highly suspect.
How could it be disinterested? The student is "evaluating" not just any
teacher, one in a class he is not enrolled in, but the very one he is
taking a class from. Not only is the student not disinterested, but the
student is very likely to be biased. The student will, most likely, have
been swayed, positively or negatively, by what the teacher has said
about his performance, either negatively or positively. This might come
out in particular cases during cross-examination, but it would be true in
all cases. The testimony of those who have reason to resent those
they accuse or who have reason to flatter them with their testimony
can have little weight. And the mere expression, as on an "evaluation"
form of "satisfaction" or "dissatisfaction" has no weight at all in an
American court. The honest expression of the feeling "I got no
satisfaction" does not prove the charge, "You didn't pay the bill," and it
does not prove the charge, "You did not teach me."
        Supposing however, that such a student, admitted as an expert, were
permitted to testify, and tried to do so anonymously, perhaps with a
white sheet over his head. Would an American court permit it? There is
no question about the answer. No court in America permits anonymous
testimony. The right of the defendant to confront the accuser is not only
in the common law but in the Constitution. The suspicion against
hearsay evidence (even when it is not anonymous) is overwhelming.
The practice of binding all witnesses by oath, or by affirmation, to tell
the truth is long-standing. The fear of later prosecution for perjury is but
one of its advantages. The cross-examining of all witnesses is another
practice of long and honored standing. All these practices, which help
in the search for truth, punish perjury, and promote justice--all these
would be compromised or wiped out if a student, or anyone, were
allowed to testify anonymously. No court in America would allow a
student to "evaluate" his teacher anonymously.
        Let us return from the tumultuous court rooms of America to the
peaceful groves of Academe.
        The main result and the intended point of "student evaluations" is to
lower the standards of the teaching faculty as a whole, to secure good
transcripts for the majority of students, and yet maintain the facade of
education. That is what "grade inflation," which every academic admits
has happened over the last twenty-five years, means: "less work, more
praise," "high grade point averages for graduates who cannot compute
an average," and "glowing letters of recommendation about students
who cannot write a letter." "Student evaluations" achieve this result
mainly by intimidation. To meet their demands, to satisfy students, the
faculty lowers its standards, usually in a series of preemptive
surrenders. In Academe as presently constituted, every division is the
rival of every other, every department the enemy of every other, every
faculty member the "colleague" of every other; all compete for students
and all receive the rewards, the "goodies," according to their success--
raises, leaves, powers, perks, parking spaces. They do not win this
war and receive the fruits of it by raising standards. Teachers will be
conscientious objectors.
        From the point of view of American justice, "student evaluations" are
secret trials, without oaths, without rules of evidence, without cross-
examination, without confrontation of witnesses, and without juries, but
not without punishments. Above all, they are through and through
secret trials by calumny.
        By being indifferent to truth, "student evaluations" encourage calumny,
and their anonymity virtually guarantees it. Where there are no false
statements, there can be no consequences for making them, no
incentive to try to avoid them, no penalties for knowingly making them,
or punishments for doing so out of malice. No Honor Code governs the
student's work on these "evaluations." Nor are the students responsible
for any of the longer consequences of their anonymous "evaluations."
They are not responsible for the uses others will put their contributions
to, for the way they may bear upon the promotion of their teacher, nor
for the long term consequence, for years and years, of tenuring or not
tenuring the teacher. The "evaluations" are commonly filled out when
students are most likely to dislike their teacher and least likely to think
of his good or the good of the college.
        In favor of the "anonymity" of "student evaluations," it is argued that
unless they are anonymous, students won't give their frank opinions,
and might meet with reprisals. Let us examine this argument. Might a
student who had criticized a teacher suffer unjust punishment from that
teacher? Human nature being what it is, that would happen. The usual
remedy in the case where a student _feels_ his or her work has been
misjudged is for the student to bring up the matter with the teacher
first; if the student is still unsatisfied, for the teacher to rejudge the
work; if the student still feels unsatisfied, for the student to appeal to
another teacher and for that third party to meet together with both
teacher and student;3 if the student is still not satisfied, some times a
standing committee of other teachers offers its counsel.4 There is no
reason to suppose this process is inadequate. It has worked well for
decades, it makes sense. There is then no reason for students to be
exempt from the requirement that their charges, criticisms, and
accusations be, like anybody else's, in their own name, and thus
subjected to the usual scrutinies that protect the innocent from
calumny. Let us look at the practice from the point of view of justice.
        There are reasons for our Courts to protect witnesses from
intimidation, kidnapping, etc.; such things as guards, sequestration, and
providing new identities later, make sense, but only _after_ a Grand
Jury has decided there is a _prima facie_ case against the Mafia boss,
he has been indicted, witnesses have been threatened by him, or the
likelihood that he will rub them out is overwhelming. Yet, note well,
even in these cases, there is no suspension of the right of the as-yet-
unconvicted boss to cross-examination and confrontation of his
accusers. Why are teachers treated less well than Mafia bosses? The
anonymity that protects student calumniators vilifies all teachers. The
mere institution of "student evaluations" was unjust to all  teachers, as
well as the enabling cause of a train of particular injustices.
        In most trials, it is possible to ask whether the person giving testimon
y
might have a motive that throws doubt on that testimony, even renders
it incredible, perhaps raises the question of perjury. Not with
"evaluations." No student takes an oath or makes an affirmation before
filling them out. (If the college has an Honor Code, it does not apply to
"evaluations.") Nor are students commonly asked questions, honest
answers to which might compromise their "evaluation" or, as the oath
does, encourage honesty. What was your reason for taking this
course? How would you describe your attitude? Could you have done
better? If so, how? How many hours a week did you work on this
course? How many times did you cut class? Nor do these "evaluations"
include questions, honest answers to which might disqualify the
"student." Did you cheat? Have you ever cheated before? Do you know
of other students who cheated? Did you turn those cheaters in? If not,
why not? As "student evaluations" are not meant to encourage the
student to examine his or her own conduct, so they do nothing to
qualify the student as a proper or even minimal judge of teaching.
Expert witnesses "students" are not.
        Moreover, if it is pointed out that the student is "evaluating" the very

person who must sometimes require them to do things that are
sometimes hard, that require exactness, good memory, desire to learn,
diligence, discipline, long practice, endurance, and long ardor, indeed
such persons who have a natural duty to require such things, if they are
to do the students good and deserve the high name of teacher--if that
is pointed out, it will only damage the teacher's defense, for it will be
taken as contempt of the court. And the teacher who refuses to read
such "evaluations," since they are calumny, which no one should
encourage by lending their ears to, will be treated as if he jumped bail.
        So much for justice as regards the students. What about the teachers?
The most obvious thing to note, is that the teachers are defenseless.
The testimony the students give, being anonymous, cannot be
examined as in a court, it cannot be cross-examined, and the students
giving it cannot be confronted. We all know that human beings are
more careful about what they say if they expect it will be examined,
especially if it may be cross-examined, and maximally, if all this will
occur in the same room with the person accused. None of these aids to
truth, checks on our perceptions, and hindrances to malice are present
in "student evaluations." Of course, the teacher cannot confront
anonymous students. Of course the teacher cannot cross-examine
anonymous accusers. What teachers are being told when they are sent
copies of the "evaluation" forms is "Here's what's being said behind
your back and it's all true." It is true that the teacher is free to question
the "evaluation" forms, but what he says will never make a dent, for the
view that student "satisfaction" is the aim of teaching enjoys
institutionally established status. In the context of Academe today, to
question that identity is to raise the suspicion that "you do not belong
here." And to do so, when evidence from "student evaluations" proves
you failed to "satisfy," is (in the eyes of those administrating things)
simply to confess not to have satisfied students, or worse, not even to
have tried. You lose.
        The standards by which an institution devoted to higher learning
practices justice ought to be higher than the market place, higher than
the polity, and even higher than the courts, not lower than all these.
Such things as calumny, blackmail, conspiracy, and lynching by
whispers ought to be not only rare in Academe, but neither winked at
by the institution, perpetrated by its officers, nor instituted in principle
by the whole. Yet "instituted in principle" precisely describes the
"student evaluations." Calumny is the practice, often envy or revenge
the motive, and intellectual indifference the rule on campuses today.
The intellectual standards of the University and the ways in which it
thinks truth is to be ascertained are inferior to the Court's, and they
may replace it. Tomorrow they would like to rule the nation.
        "Student evaluations" of teachers, if carried into the polity, will chan
ge
many cherished things. All our old protections, built up over a thousand
years, will be swept away. If anonymous "student evaluations" of
teachers are made the model of justice at large, we may expect our
Sixth Amendment, which allows no witness to testify against us without
confronting us, in person, in the same room, to be "interpreted" away.
No American court allows a witness against the defendant to escape
cross-examination, by the defendant or his lawyer, but since this
certainly might have what Academic administrators call "a chilling
effect," it will be righteously denounced and swiftly eliminated. Our
courts require witnesses to swear or affirm the truth of their
statements and to suffer penalties for perjury. It will be said, "But this
exposes witnesses to risk, it should be removed," and we will lose this
ancient guard of our liberty. Again, our courts insist on good manners,
cooperation, and respect, and they cite trespassers for "contempt of
court." "Out of date, fussy, uptight" will be the cry denouncing this
practice. Our courts restrict opinion from witnesses, are chary of
inferences, and prefer first-hand testimony about facts. "Free speech"
will be the cry against these restrictions. No American court admits
hearsay evidence, except under special, unusual, and limited
circumstances.5 "Isn't it a free country?" will be the cunning plea
against these standards. Standards for expert witnesses exist, such
that no student, only a fellow teacher, would be deemed an expert on
teaching in an American court.6 "How arrogant" it will be exclaimed,
"for any one to claim to be better than another," and all the standards
on expert testimony will vanish. Judges become judges through long
training and experience and they serve "on good behavior" and can, in
most states and the whole federal system, only be removed by
impeachment and trial. But why should that be? If they are to be so
"tenured," why not let defendants have a say in the matter. Let us have
anonymous "evaluations" of judges. Indeed, why not have all judges
elected and only by the cross-section of the citizenry that appears in
the docket?7
        If the "student evaluations" that now characterize Academe's
understanding of justice should ever invade America, all the old
protections of our liberties will have to go. But after all, many of the
most sophisticated minds in Academe today proclaim there is no truth,
only opinion. Make no mistake, where truth itself is calumniated,
something will be put in its place. Our Fifth Amendment protects us
from trials without due process. But what is this when we weigh in the
balance the "right to satisfaction" that jurists educated in the new
Academe will find in the penumbra of the shadow of some sentence not
in the Constitution or the Declaration? That accomplished, there will be
little pursuit of happiness in this land.
        The most notable other institutions in our time to pass laws
encouraging and coercing calumny, including children against their
parents, are the Soviet and Chinese Communist regimes. That these
tyrannies are abhorrent, that all tyrannies from the Caesars of old to
the latest Idi Amins and Pol Pots have employed secret informers, and
that precisely the most earnest, benevolent, and gentle teachers of
humanity, Socrates and Christ, were destroyed by calumny--these are
among the considerations that ought to persuade intelligent persons to
abolish such anonymous "evaluations" in Academe and convince free
citizens in America to beware if they do not.
        The current practices of Academe pose a question to the American
people: Will Academe teach its new principles to America, or will
America once again assert the rule of its principles everywhere in the
Union? Those principles were announced in the Declaration, secured by
the War of Independence, framed in the Constitution, and extended
during the Civil War by the blood of our forefathers. As was said of old,
a house divided cannot stand. It will become either all one thing, or all
another. Either Academe will spread the rule of calumny everywhere in
America or America will bring the rule of reason to Academe. ##
___________________________________________
