University Centers for Rational Alternatives
_Measure_ Number 112, January 1993

LETTERS TO THE EDITOR

Dear Sir:

        Professor Gribben's account presents no surprises to anyone in
today's academy in this country; it is if anything an account of routine
and quite everyday conduct of professors. It makes his indignation no
less righteous to note that that's pretty much how things are in some
places--but not all. I went through the same range of experiences at
Brown University from May, 1981, until I left in sheer disgust at a
ruined university eight years later. But the issue was hardly politics at
all. It was merely my stated opposition to the systematic dismantling of
a once excellent research university by mediocre professors.
Specifically, I announced in the student paper that professors who
coddle students do them no favors. I wrote that unexceptional
sentiment in dramatic form--"a commencement speech you'll never
hear,"--but it was heard throughout the world, because the students
verbally lynched me for saying so. My department exacted its
vengeance. I found myself denied existence; the Department of
Religious Studies at Brown made an appointment in my field, about
which I was not even informed until the person started getting her mail
in the office. Through the rumor mills I was broadly discredited
throughout the country. I was subjected to a campaign of
disinformation: out and out lying and misrepresentation by department
colleagues. Students were warned to keep away; relationships with
them were systematically subverted. Collegiality, truth, standards,
were discarded. The Department called meetings and excluded me;
reached tenure decisions without counting my vote. The chairman
refused to permit me to give courses of my own choosing, though I
held the rank of University Professor. The sad story follows its own
lines, but they run parallel to those of Professor Gribben's account. So
I found for myself a university that runs by rules in a university system
that maintains high standards and take pride in its work.
        When venal provosts and political presidents permit or even encourage
the de-professionalization of their institutions, as was the case at
Brown and Texas at Austin, these things happen; the future of those
schools is blighted. But Professor Gribben's career and mine also
attest that not all universities protect mediocrity and celebrate personal
preferment, inclusive of this morning's political prejudices. One of the
real pleasures of life is surviving, and Professor Gribben and many like
him enjoy their triumph in the success of lives and careers left, if not
intact, al least unimpaired. Success is the best revenge. Now let's get
on with the real work of the academy, which is scholarship, in the form
of teaching and writing alike.

Sincerely,

Jacob Neusner
Distinguished Research Professor of Religious Studies
University of South Florida

