tk0jut1@mp.cs.niu.edu (jim thomas) wrote:
>In article <C1wAH2.8GC@ms.uky.edu> morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) writes:
>
>>You are issued a PC by the university.  It is not your property; it
>>has merely been issued to your for your work.  If the software in-
>>stalled on that system comes from a university site license (as op-
>>posed to the copy you purchased for personal use), it belongs to the
>>University as well.
>
>There has been overwhelming discussion on this topic over the past year
>indicating the issue is far less simple than your statements suggest.

Quite frankly, the "common sense" approach is extremely simple.  In fact,
I can state my position (in this situation) in three sentences:

	1) If the hardware is theirs, they have a right and/or obligation
           to ensure that it is maintained properly (no hardware hacks or
           damage to the system).
	2) If the software is licensed to them, they have a right and/or 
           obligation to ensure that it is maintained properly (no illicit 
           copies or distrbution)
	3) Neither 1) nor 2) imply any control or (or access to) user
	   data files (such as spreadsheets, documents, etc.)

They provide the software/hardware; what I do with it is *usually* my
business.  There's the disinction between property rights and privacy
or academic freedom issues.  Neither privacy nor academic freedom ex-
tends to misuse of software licensed to the University.

>Can you provide pointers to any source that provides for such
>unlimited searches as you suggest? The fact that a PC is not *MY* property
>does not necessarily grant to the University unlimited access to it.

I did not suggest that the University had "unlimited access" to the
PC on my desk.  I said that they owned the PC and <usually> some/most
of the software.  I never said that they could/should review user data.

>You seem to confuse the meaning of "maintained" and "operated in
>accordance with." Neither meaning requires university intrusion unless
>explicitly pre-authorized in a user agreement. The university has even
>less right to search my University-provided PC (if I had one, which I
>do not) than to scour my University-provided files for evidence of
>potential wrong-doing. 

I have never suggested that the University had the right to search/scour
*every file* on one of their PCs.  I said that they had the right to verify
that their licenses were being used properly; that's a big distinction.  Any
University official who started riffling through my spreadsheets or documents
would get a rather large amount of flak; however, I have no problem with a
verification of the existence/licensing of WP.EXE or QPRO.EXE.

>Karl (and others) have provided pointers to
>legal and administrative sources protecting privacy, and your assertions,
>while passionate, does not alter those protections. 

Privacy rules apply to my files, not theirs, eh?  Data files (spreadsheets,
documents, et cetera) are mine; the program(s) which generated them, however,
are (usually) the University's.  I'll be the first to admit that it's another
tightrope, but there *is* a difference between the two.

>>I would equate a "legitimate software" check with a dormitory room
>>inspection or a laboratory inspection; the university has the right 
>>to ensure that you're using their equipment properly.
>>
>The university has no right to search through one's personal effects.

Agreed.

>They may open the door of my room, scope out the landscape, and engage
>in non-obtrusive observation. The may not intrude into my most personal
>of spaces that are not in plain view without authority or my permission,
>neither of which can be assumed simply by U. "ownership."

Absolutely not!  I agree completely; neither a dormitory inspection nor
a laboratory inspection (here at UK, that is) involves a review of per-
sonal effects; they use the same "observation" technique that you mention.

Wouldn't a check of WP.EXE be non-intrusive?  After all, they aren't looking
at your personal data, right?

>Would you allow intrusion into a university computer for one
>violating "speech" rather than "property" infractions?

No, I wouldn't.

>It is reasonable to expect that university staff will abide by existing
>laws in using university equipment. It is unreasonable to expect the
>university to engage in intrusive measures to assure that I do so.

I agree with you, believe it or not.  We merely differ in our definition
of "intrusive".  What would you think of a program that:
	- Contained a database of University-licensed software
	- Searched for that software (and *only* that software)
	- Displayed a list of findings
I wouldn't call that "intrusive", since it doesn't reveal the content
(or even the existence) of user data; it merely looks for that soft-
ware which belongs to the University.  It *is* possible to maintain
control of University property (the software itself) without compro-
mising the confidentiality of your property (the data files generated
by that software).

--Wes
