In article <1993Feb4.025334.5350@netcom.com>, nagle@netcom.com (John Nagle) writes:
> morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) writes:
>>Replace "ftp server" with "public library" for a moment.  While the 
>>library considers membership/borrowing information confidential, it
>>certainly has many in-house uses.  I doubt that the "domestic intel-
>>ligence agencies" have much use for borrowers' records.
> 
>       This is incorrect.  The FBI had at one time a "library awareness
> program", under which libraries were requested to report patrons 
> accessing certain information (primarily involving weapons technology)
> to the FBI.  This generated considerable controversy, and after strong
> protests from the American Library Association, it was cancelled.
> The ALA now has taken the position that circulation records should be
> destroyed once the loaned materials to which they refer have
> been returned.

One important point about the Library Awareness Program is the FBI didn't
ask for (or get) access to borrower records (those are normally protected
under state law, requiring court orders for their release).  It seemed to
be more like a "Neighborhood Awareness Program", warning the residents
(librarians) that there are "bad" people out there and to report any
suspicious people looking for information.  I guess the FBI forgot the
purpose of a library is to help people find information (resulting
in a major culture clash).

So the keeping, or destruction of circulation records really doesn't have
much bearing the Library Awareness Program.  Also, I never heard of any
professional librarian actually telling the FBI anything because of the
library awareness program.  There were also other problems with the program,
such as identifying "foreign looking people" (insert your favorite ethinic or
racial group) as requiring special watching which caused other organizations
besides the ALA to also oppose it.

While library records have special protection in many states, that
doesn't mean the information is always kept secret.  In some cities
the library computers and the police department computers are both
in the same computer center maintained by the same city DP staff.
Sometimes the staff make mistakes and release information they shouldn't.
"Hi, I didn't get a notice for this book, can you tell me what address 
you have down for John Smith in your computer.  Oh, sure let me look
that up... it says 1234 somestreet."

Of course court orders can get the information.  Cases like the Zodiac
serial murders get a lot of press attention when the police wanted to know
everyone who had checked out a certain book (the library did turn over
their records under court order).  But most of the court orders seem to
be in divorce cases.  Usually resulting in a librarian having to appear
in court to explain why the library can't produce the records.  Judges
seem to think it is contempt when you tell them the records are destroyed
automatically when the book is returned.

In some manual circulation systems the names of the people who had checked
out a book where written on a slip of paper in a pocket in the book.  While
the book was checked out the card was kept in a box at the counter, and
replaced in the book when it was returned.  I was in one small library that
didn't even have a librarian on duty.  They just had a book on the counter
that you wrote your name, and the title of the book (of course you could
flip through the pages and see all the books checked out by other people).

Then there are faculty members who believe they should have access to the
library records.  Things like who checked out the material they put on reserve
at the library for their class.  Or they suspect a student of academic
dishonesty (plagiarism) and want a list of the books checked out by the
student.  After all they are university employees, and those are university
records, and unversity books.  Or the ever popular, "Well, the computer
center didn't have a problem restoring their backup tapes when I wanted
to check the student's files, so why won't the library show me the list
of books checked out."

These things vary, so you should check with your own library to see exactly
what their policies are and who they give access to their records.  Don't
assume that every state, or university has the same policies.


> 
>       Given this history, information servers operated by libraries
> should not log access.

That one is going to be tough.  Libraries keep a lot of records about
how their resources are used, so they can go to their funding agency
and say what a great job they are doing.  Libraries don't charge for
a lot of their services, so they can't show how much money they are
making.  Instead they count everything.  Every reference question
answered, every phone call, every book checked out, every magazine
left on a table, every person who walks in the door, ....  are all
counted.

Even now there are libraries with online catalogs that keep track of
every search done on their computers (most of the systems used by major
academic libraries have this capability).  Librarians use this information
for everything from adding cross-references to the files based on popular
usage (Movies  -> see Motion Pictures) to writing research papers on how
people search.  There were a couple of presentations at the last ALA meeting
about how libraries can better use their public access transaction logs.

While librarians try to prevent any of the information from being
personally identifiable, technology keeps changing and sometimes by
matching the information with other information one can identify a
person.  When there was only access from public terminals in the
library, logging the terminal identifier didn't identify the person.
But with campus-wide network access, sometimes logging the network
address identifies a personal computer used by only one person.  The
Census Bureau sometimes has the same problem with their data.

If you don't return a book, all bets are off.  Some libraries will
turn your name over to the police, collection agencies, the burser's
office, etc.  I know of one library that loses 80,000 books a year, so
they don't have much of a sense of humor about it.  Its your tax money,
imagine how many Internet connections that could buy.
