From caf-talk Caf Feb  5 02:04:41 1993
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,news.admin.policy
Message-ID: <IfQReoK00iV009yqQg@andrew.cmu.edu>
Date: Thu,  4 Feb 1993 22:10:44 -0500 
From: Nicholas Kramer <nk24+@andrew.cmu.edu>
Subject: Re: FAST CASH!!!

Excerpts from netnews.news.admin.policy: 4-Feb-93 Re: FAST CASH!!! by
Carl M. Kadie@eff.org 
> Schools have something better than bail, the ability to withhold
> transcripts.

 As I see it, there are two purposes to bail. The stated purpose is to
keep the criminal from fleeing and missing trial. That's really not a
problem in an academic setting, and the ability to withhold transcripts
merely reinforces that.

 The other, unstated purpose is to compare a person's needs for freedom
(/computer usage) versus society's wish to restrain him/her. It's a "put
your money where your mouth is" concept. If a person is accused of
something really serious, but the accused is equally serious about
remaining on the net, he'll put down the cash. If, however, he doesn't
see net access as very important, he won't post bail.

 Although much of today's judicial system is a farce, I think this is
one of the parts that works reasonably well. Why not extend it to
similar situations in universities?


						Nick



