kadie@eff.org (Carl Kadie) writes:

>}I think a search is when 1) there is no notice of the time of the
>}search/inspection or 2) no notice of exact nature of the
>}search/inspection or 3) there is a change that personal material will
>}be seen by people.

john@iastate.edu (John Hascall) writes:

>I think "3)" is *way* too broad.  In a physical search/inspection,
>you could hardly avoid seeing personal material.

My statement was unclear. If my dorm room had been subject to a fire
safety inspection, I could have confidence that the inspectors would
not read the papers on my desk because that was outside the scope of
the inspection. While I could could not assume that they would not see
the embarrassing posters on my walls, I could remove those posters
before the inspection if I had wanted to because I had been given
notice.

In the computer domain. the privacy of personal material could be
protected by not having inspections. Or by asking people "inspect
themselves" (the policy at U. of Oregon). Or, I suppose, by using an
automated inspection program and by making the inspection program
publically available so that people could verify for themselves that
it will not register any false positives on personal material. Or, I
suppose, with a procedure such as:
    Turn on the computer
    Type "123", record registration name and number.


>Also, do you think signing something which says "at any time",
>covers "a notice of the time"

No. I think notice of time should be specific.

[Historical note. According to a documentary I saw on PBS last year,
 in U.S. Colonial times search warrants were "general"; that is, not
 limited to a specific place or time. The lasted for the life of the
 King. The 4th Amendment's requirement of specificity was a reaction
 to the general warrants.]

>.  And similarly, "to insure
>compliance with licenses and copyright" for "exact nature"?
[...]

No. That would allow email archives to be read (for evidence of
discussions of illegal copying.) The procedure used in the search
should be specified (e.g. we are going to run this program and record
its output). The search procedure should not involve a human reading
personal material.

- Carl


