After some thought (and a few email exchanges; thanks, guys!), I have 
a suggestion.

We've ascertained (and SPA assumes) that a University has some
responsibility and/or liability for the software loaded/used on
its PCs.  Therefore, we cannot take an "anything goes" attitude.

However, several participants in this discussion feel that any
inspection/search of a user's PC, regardless of its University 
ownership, is tantamount to illegal search and seizure.

What if (in the future) we established a policy that regulated
the installation of third-party software on University-owned
systems?  Something like this comes to mind:

	- Require the user to furnish a copy of the license (or 
	  purchase order) for each piece of software *before*
	  installing it on his University-owned PC.  

	- If the proper certification (license/PO) is provided, the 
	  user shall not be prevented from installing the software.
	  (This prevents some overzealous bureaucrat from preventing
	  the installation of controversial programs.)

	- The University will make periodic *announced* inspections; any 
	  software whose certification has not been submitted will be
	  removed if certification could not be provided.  (This would 
	  ONLY pertain to the programs themselves; user data files would not 
	  be inspected.)
	
This scheme accomplishes several goals:

	- The user doesn't worry about unannounced inspections (unless
	  the SPA barges through the door)

	- The university has a demonstrated policy/procedure for cur-
	  tailment of "institutional piracy".

	- The university's rear is covered if the SPA comes to town,
	  since the liability for any unlicensed software can be shown
	  to lie directly with the user (assuming that one of those
	  periodic inspections was performed recently; I would think
	  that a monthly/bimonthly inspection would be sufficient).

	- The University has protected its interest in its property.
	  (in many cases, this is *both* the hardware and software.)

	- This doesn't really add much to the user's responsibility;
	  most of the software purchased by faculty/staff is purchased
	  through the university, so the purchasing records are already
	  on hand; the user doesn't have to dig up every copy of every
	  license.)

This policy could be incorporated into an agreement signed by the person
responsible for each PC *when it is issued to them*.

What do you think?

--Wes

