In article <1993Feb5.153308.17790@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:

>As one of "the people", the 4th Amendment guarantees my right to be
>secure in my person, my papers, and my effects against government
>intrusion.

Yes. Is the claim here that because U of O is a tax supported
university that it, and  its employees are all "the government"? 

>> My first point is that the property
>>in question is U of O's property. Not your property. Your right to
>>YOUR privacy only applies to YOUR effects
>
>My right to privacy from the government applies to my person, my
>papers, and my effects, even if they reside on public property such as
>a dorm room, assigned office space, desk, and computer.

A dorm room, assigned office space, desk and computer are not "public
property" they are PRIVATE property of another individual or
organization, in this case the UofO. They let you use them as part of
an overall exchange of value between you, probably contractually
governed. It is subject to agreement and value received on both sides.
In the case of a dorm room, you agree to pay the room (and board) fee.
This is the value the University receives from you. The benefit you
receive is the place to reside while attending school. The agreement
between you in most cases is in writing, and is tantamount to a
landlord-tenant rental agreement.  Just as in rental cases, the
landlord has some legal responsibilities for the saftey, and legality
of the building, its contents, and acts that take place within it.
Courts MAY allow the owners some leeway about what are REASONBLE steps
to take to protect it from legal entanglements initiated by tenants.
So not ALL responsibility devolves on the owner, and the owner may use
the contract to more clearly draw that line with tenants who accept
that contract.  The contract may also delineate property rights that
the owner wants to retain (such as the right to inspect their (not
your) property). If you don't like that, you can choose not to enter
into their contract.  It is your choice depending upon your view of
the exchange of value, restrictions as well. 

Now, their right to access and inspect THEIR property doesn't give
them the right to search your property, unless you agreed to that too
as part of your contract. It may be inconvenient for you to remove
your personal property from inside their property when they want to
make inspections and you want to keep your property private. But
that's an inconvenience you face because you chose to store your stuff
inside their property instead of inside a container of your own.

I do agree that there are many ways (setting up standard inspection
times, or advance notice periods, or standard inspection programs)
that can do this less intrusively. And it might be smart as a consumer
to ask that the contract be ammended to specify these to increase the
relative value you receive to the relative cost you pay. But I don't
believe the fourth amendment requires that they do so.  If they are
unreasonable you can always go and do a deal with someone else more
reasonable. This is what I meant about the 4th amendment not limiting
what a person can do contractually when they agree to lend or rent
their property to someone else.

