Wes Morgan makes several good points in challenging my view that
my university has the absolute right to inspect my computer.
However, I strongly disagree with his view that such an inspection
is non-intrusive. Consider (assuming the PC is in my school office):
 
1) Their right to inspect could be a condition of use. Although I would
not accept such a condition, I agree that such a condition--if I accepted
it--would be change things. Normally, PCs, as with offices, desks, and
other university-provided resources, allow for considerable discretion
in using them for reasonable personal use (hiding birthday presents in
a file cabinet, tax records in a desk, love letters in a folder,
non-academic books on the shelves). I would expect the same latitude
with a computer (quicken for my checks, running the office baseball
pool from dBase). To invade my computer is to invade my personal life.
 
2) The only way my computer could be searched/inspected would be to
actually invade it. There is no easy way to search a computer without
a directory manager of some kind, and there are sufficient means of
hiding programs, directories (and even drives) that a search/inspection
would require more than a simple dir command.
 
3) The SPA's SPAUDIT, while a useful preliminary tool, is unreliable.
In using it on my computer it identified programs that were and had never
been installed. A "hit" would require the inspector to dig more fully
into my directories, perhaps even files. This is intrusive.
 
"Public" computers (such as those in a computer lab) should certainly be
inspected regularly. However, faculty PCs allocated for personal use by 
that faculty, should not.
 
I have two PCs in my home. I have purchased as site license for Word Perfect.
I installed the program on one PC (the other uses an old version of
Word Start that came with the computer). Under the logic of absolute right
to inspection, would you also give the U the right to periodically
monitor both home computers to assure I wasn't double-dipping?
 
As the one who seems to have inherited (inter alia) the MIS functions in
our department, I consider it an absolute obligation to inspect the
"public" computers in our lab and wipe out any and all programs, whether
shareware or conventional, that had no evidence of purchase. However,
I would under no circumstances inspect a university-provided PC in
a colleague's office. 
 
Although I strongly disagree with your position, it suggests one issue
I had not thought of and should implement: What policies should guide the
privacy relationship between the user and provider? My own view is that
the general university policies governing software use are sufficient.
 
Jim Thomas


