Brian, I have to make a correction here.  This is not the first time
the Schedule of Classes data has been available online.

I first received the data in the fall semester of 1987 for an XCF
project.  I wrote a nice front-end that let anyone query the schedule
database with any combination of criteria.  It used the same protocol
as 'pb', the old campus phone book program.  The data was stored in
an Ingres database.

There were no technical problems with maintaining, updating and
distributing this information, but there were tremendous
political/adminstrative problems.

The data was "owned" by OAR, the Office of Admissions and Records, but
it was maintained by an IS department in Banway.  At that time the
"ownership" of the data had been recently fought over by various groups
from the Academic Senate to OAR.  I had to get forms signed by the
Banway IS group, the Registrar (Dr. Bailey), the Vice-Chancellor
for Computing Resources (Prof. Hardyck), the XCF advisor (Prof.
Newton), the XCF director and a couple of miscellaneous OAR folks.

I also had to agree that under no circumstances would I let CF&C
(Computer Facilities & Communications), a competing campus IS group,
now named CCS, get control of my project.  

The political battle was ridiculous.  The first semester's data wasn't
available to the public until November of that year due to all the
infighting.  The battle started all over again for the next semester.
The spring semester data was finally received in April (for a term
that begin 3 months earlier).

At this point I gave up on the whole project.  I was simultaneously
working on getting the Course Catalog data, but in a full year of
bureaucracy I was unable to get that data released.  The Academic
Senate had control of it, but no Senate committee had the right
(or willingness) to make it available.

Never underestimate the petty power battles that can be waged over
"public" data.  I'm overjoyed that the University has finally made
the schedule available.  It only took them 5 years from the time
I put the first schedule on the net to get the internal struggles
resolved.  Maybe someday they'll release the course catalog as well.

