The age-old technical means of protecting software from being "stolen"
is the copy protection scheme.

Traditionally, these have been horrid failures, prone more to confuse
customers than to prevent the Dreaded Software Pirate (who gets a
cracked copy of 1-2-3 from the local pirate BBS outlet anyways).  The
average software package nowadays does not come copy-protected, but with
the popularity of networks, there is growing use of "license-protection"
schemes.  Some examples are the Ethernet-number key-code system, network
license broadcast/scan, the license database server, and others.

On this campus, there is a generalized "key-server" network system.  A
server on the network holds tables that determines under what conditions
a program can be used.  This server is queried by a stub program that
heads off the hacked-up code of an application (say, MacWrite).  If the
server determines that this computer is Worthy, it sends the proper code
along.

The Univeristy can alter any program to work with this key server,
allowing them to make lots of copies of the hacked program available for
anyone to use (without worrying about misuse).  It's a non-invasive way
of insuring that "correct use" goes on, and helps to secure further site
licenses of software.  

The reason it works so well on this campus is because nearly every
computer is networked (most of them being Macintoshes hooked up in dorm
rooms).  The disadvantage is that if you yank your network plug (say, to
take your computer to a conference), your software suddenly stops
working.




