In article <C1wEDA.7CF@cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M. Kadie) writes:
>At least to my reading, the 4th Amendment is about privacy more than
>property. It says: "The right of the people to be secure in their
>persons, [...] papers, and effects, against unreasonable searches and
>seizures, shall not be violated;"

But what is an "unreasonable" search?  Searching a multi-user computer, or
a computer whose filesystems appear on a network, can be done without placing
any burden on the user of the computer.  Properly done (e.g., from a batch
process running when the machine is idle), the user would not even be aware
that the machine would be searched.

In fact, one could easily incorportate the search into the site's backup
procedure.  Going a step farther, suppose a site searched the backup tapes
for improper software, and only checked the machines to verify things found
on the backup tapes?  In addition to finding improper software, this would
provide a safety check to make sure that the backup tapes are still readable.
One could argue that this is not only not an unreasonable search, it is
actually a benificial search!

Or how about a search that is done while doing a search for other purposes?
For example, suppose a site ran a program periodically that searched for
duplicate files, so that people with duplicate files (e.g., multiple copies of
the same GIF or large software distribution or whatever) could be automatically
sent email asking them to try to cooperate so that only one copy is kept
online.  Would this be an acceptable search?  If so, then an addition check
that looked for unauthorized software would not turn it into an unreasonable
search, would it?

--Tim Smith

