In article <RSM.93Feb7005433@coral.math.arizona.edu> rsm@math.arizona.edu (Robert S. Maier) writes:
>After reading about the bizarre response by the people at SRI to a harmless
>attempt to ftp their files, I thought I'd ftp to ai.sri.com myself and
>check it out.
>
>I found the following egregious announcement in ~ftp/pub/README:
>
>	Non-SRI people: This is *NOT* a public directory.  If you are able
>	to access a file in this (or any other directory on a *.AI.SRI.COM
>	host), you are not automatically allowed to retrieve, read, or
>	otherwise use that file.  In order to do so, you must have the
>	prior permission of SRI.

Okay, I'll agree: this notice is definitely bizarre for an anonymous
ftp archive.

I'm not sure whether making something available via anonymous ftp and 
then "protecting" it via a notice would be considered due diligence.
I certainly would not entrust valued intellectual property to such
a system.

On the other hand, how they choose to protect their information is 
their choice (no matter how esoteric).  Sending an informative email 
message to them pointing out that the policy is bound to be ignored 
is useful; barraging them with indignant mail over "the traditions
of anonymous ftp" is unreasonable.  You can't expect everyone to
meet your expectations, and using mass email to bludgeon them into 
submission does not respect their right to operate _their_ system 
as they see fit while not harming others.

Note that there are other machines on the Internet with "interesting"
access policies.  Some of these policies orginate in federal or treaty
law, and as much as we may like, they're not going to go away just 
because we'd like all archives to be treated the same.

I'll follow SRI's guidelines when accessing their system.  The message,
while unusual, clearly conveys their policy.  To violate it intentionally
is not justified.

/John

