Carl, I just ftp'd and read, per your suggestion, the student's
original posting (from ftp.eff.org pub/academic/batch/feb_07_1993),
and your statement above is rather misleading, in my opinion.

The student states, categorically, that he did a 'GET password' 
to look at a file of that or a similar name on the SRI machine.
He then describes the file he got as an encrypted file.

His roaming around looking for interesting AI documents may
well have been perfectly innocent, but any sysadmin who doesn't
followup a log item of an anonymous login ftp'ing a file with
the name 'password' in it is, again in my opinion, a damned idiot.

I should note that I am a long-time employee of SRI International,
although never a user of AI.SRI.COM and not involved in SRI
computer security.  My remarks and opinions reflect my own
point of view, not SRI's.

Having said that, a sysadmin in an organization that is
frequently "attacked" by crackers is very much within his
rights and responsibilities to followup on an apparent
attempt to ftp what was apparently a password file.  That is usually
the first order of attack on a system, i.e., downloading
the password file and attempting to decrypt the weak passwords.

The student should have known better than going after password
files; perhaps his instructors have been at fault for not
mentioning this matter in using the network.

That is, in my opinion, the telephone call was justified.

[I have no opinion whatsoever about the meaning or legitimacy
of the README file in the ftp directory on AI.SRI.COM.]

